# test/mutants - the corpus: each record breaks ONE guard on purpose, and
# names the test(s) that must notice.
#
# WHY THIS IS A FILE AND NOT A HABIT. Every defect this project has found was
# found by someone remembering to apply a discipline, and the discipline that
# matters most -- "confirm the guard actually bites" -- has failed repeatedly,
# always in my own hands rather than the code's:
#
#   a search string that never matched, so the file was untouched and the test
#   correctly passed, which read as "the guard does not bite";
#   a sed delimiter that clashed with `||`, so the mutation never applied;
#   a SIGKILL backstop that looked covered because the test hook died politely
#   on SIGTERM, making plain `timeout` and `-k` indistinguishable;
#   a mutation that produced UNBALANCED SHELL, so it "killed" on a syntax
#   error and proved nothing at all.
#
# So the driver asserts the mutation LANDED, that the result still PARSES, that
# the named tests pass BEFORE it, and only then that one dies. A guard nothing
# kills is decorative, and this file turns that from a thing to remember into a
# thing that fails.
#
# FORMAT, one record per block, blank line between:
#
#   = <name>
#   f <file, repo-relative>
#   t <test names, space separated, without the .t>
#   - <the exact line to remove>
#   + <the line to put in its place; omit the whole line to just delete>
#
# Matching is EXACT and whole-line, first occurrence: no regex, no delimiters,
# nothing to clash with `||` or `*` or a slash. Two of the failures above were
# pattern-syntax accidents, and an exact match can only be right or absent --
# and absent is a hard ERROR, never a silent skip. Leading whitespace after the
# two-character prefix is significant and is preserved exactly.

